04-01
00:02:36.077 --> 00:02:40.579
Start with questions A one to A three on page one of the worksheet.

04-02
00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:49.930
First, define the four quantities: liquid composition, vapor
composition, overall composition, and activity coefficient.

04-03
00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:56.361
Be precise about which quantities describe a phase and which describe
the entire mixture.

04-04
00:02:56.541 --> 00:03:00.871
For A two, use the numerical observation printed on the slide.

04-05
00:03:01.051 --> 00:03:10.353
Multiply vapor composition by total pressure, then divide by liquid
composition times the pure-component saturation pressure.

04-06
00:03:10.533 --> 00:03:13.925
Use the full worksheet values before rounding.

04-07
00:03:14.105 --> 00:03:17.987
The pressures use the same units, so those units cancel.

04-08
00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:24.439
The stated assumptions are ideal vapor behavior and a negligible
Poynting correction.

04-09
00:03:24.619 --> 00:03:31.766
For A three, write a short explanation of whether a small fitting
error proves thermodynamic consistency.

04-10
00:03:31.946 --> 00:03:35.615
Pause the video now and attempt all three questions.

04-11
00:03:35.795 --> 00:03:39.827
Allow about nine minutes, then resume to check your work.

05-01
00:03:42.357 --> 00:03:44.427
Check your definitions first.

05-02
00:03:44.607 --> 00:03:49.428
Liquid composition, x one, is a mole fraction within the liquid.

05-03
00:03:49.608 --> 00:03:54.493
Vapor composition, y one, is a mole fraction within the vapor.

05-04
00:03:54.673 --> 00:04:00.113
Overall composition, z one, describes the combined feed or mixture.

05-05
00:04:00.293 --> 00:04:03.067
These do not specify phase amounts.

05-06
00:04:03.247 --> 00:04:09.625
With the pure-liquid standard state, activity is liquid mole fraction
multiplied by gamma.

05-07
00:04:09.805 --> 00:04:15.651
Gamma is dimensionless and measures the departure from the ideal-
mixture activity.

05-08
00:04:15.831 --> 00:04:21.676
Your calculation should give approximately one point four nine one
five four five.

05-09
00:04:21.856 --> 00:04:28.192
Its natural logarithm is approximately zero point three nine nine
eight one three.

05-10
00:04:28.372 --> 00:04:32.041
Gamma is not a composition or a phase fraction.

05-11
00:04:32.221 --> 00:04:37.875
For A three, fitting asks how well a chosen model reproduces the
observations.

05-12
00:04:38.055 --> 00:04:48.359
Consistency asks whether properties inferred from those observations
satisfy a thermodynamic constraint, using the appropriate assumptions.

05-13
00:04:48.539 --> 00:04:53.168
A small fitting error alone does not answer that second question.

06-01
00:04:55.698 --> 00:04:59.922
To understand the constraint, we need one energy function.

06-02
00:05:00.102 --> 00:05:10.129
Excess Gibbs energy measures the difference between the actual mixture
and an ideal mixture at the same temperature, pressure, and
composition.

06-03
00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:13.743
It is not the entire Gibbs energy of mixing.

06-04
00:05:13.923 --> 00:05:19.193
The ideal contribution will also matter when we examine stability
later.

06-05
00:05:19.373 --> 00:05:28.738
The partial molar excess Gibbs energy of a component is R T times the
natural logarithm of its activity coefficient.

06-06
00:05:28.918 --> 00:05:36.555
The mixture's molar excess Gibbs energy is the composition-weighted
sum of those partial molar quantities.

06-07
00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:43.647
Dividing by R T gives the dimensionless function shown here, which we
will call g tilde.

06-08
00:05:43.827 --> 00:05:48.926
For a binary mixture, take x as the mole fraction of component one.

06-09
00:05:49.106 --> 00:05:55.844
Then g tilde equals x times log gamma one, plus one minus x times log
gamma two.

06-10
00:05:56.024 --> 00:05:59.566
Every log here means the natural logarithm.

06-11
00:05:59.746 --> 00:06:01.431
The weights add to one.

06-12
00:06:01.611 --> 00:06:07.691
Both activity coefficients describe partial molar information from the
same energy function.

06-13
00:06:07.871 --> 00:06:11.839
They therefore cannot be selected as two unrelated curves.

06-14
00:06:12.019 --> 00:06:14.984
Use this definition to begin B one.

07-01
00:06:17.514 --> 00:06:25.514
At fixed temperature and pressure, the Gibbs-Duhem equation tells us
how the two activity coefficients must change together.

07-02
00:06:25.694 --> 00:06:29.129
Multiply the change in log gamma one by x.

07-03
00:06:29.309 --> 00:06:33.832
Multiply the change in log gamma two by one minus x.

07-04
00:06:34.012 --> 00:06:35.804
The sum must be zero.

07-05
00:06:35.984 --> 00:06:44.197
These are composition-weighted changes, not an instruction that the
activity coefficients themselves must add to one.

07-06
00:06:44.377 --> 00:06:54.489
At an interior composition, if one log activity coefficient increases
as composition changes, the other must change in a compatible way.

07-07
00:06:54.669 --> 00:06:58.573
The weights determine the relationship between those changes.

07-08
00:06:58.753 --> 00:07:02.294
An ideal mixture is a simple special case.

07-09
00:07:02.474 --> 00:07:08.981
Both gamma values equal one, both logarithms are zero, and the
condition is satisfied.

07-10
00:07:09.161 --> 00:07:13.385
But a nonideal mixture can also satisfy Gibbs-Duhem.

07-11
00:07:13.565 --> 00:07:19.193
Large gamma values do not, by themselves, indicate inconsistent data.

07-12
00:07:19.373 --> 00:07:24.088
What matters is the relationship between their composition
dependences.

08-01
00:07:26.618 --> 00:07:30.948
Now differentiate g tilde with respect to composition.

08-02
00:07:31.128 --> 00:07:36.078
Use the product rule: both the weights and the logarithms depend on x.

08-03
00:07:36.258 --> 00:07:45.346
Differentiating x times log gamma one produces log gamma one, plus x
times the derivative of log gamma one.

08-04
00:07:45.526 --> 00:07:56.704
Differentiating one minus x times log gamma two produces minus log
gamma two, plus one minus x times the derivative of log gamma two.

08-05
00:07:56.884 --> 00:07:59.679
Collect the two plain logarithms first.

08-06
00:07:59.859 --> 00:08:05.278
Their difference is the natural logarithm of gamma one divided by
gamma two.

08-07
00:08:05.458 --> 00:08:07.356
We call that quantity L.

08-08
00:08:07.536 --> 00:08:11.803
The remaining terms are the composition-weighted derivatives.

08-09
00:08:11.983 --> 00:08:16.484
At fixed temperature and pressure, Gibbs-Duhem makes their sum zero.

08-10
00:08:16.664 --> 00:08:22.446
The result is compact: the slope of g tilde equals L at the same
composition.

08-11
00:08:22.626 --> 00:08:25.890
This gives us two ways to describe the same slope.

08-12
00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:34.475
We can estimate it from neighboring excess-energy values, or calculate
L from the activity coefficients at the center.

08-13
00:08:34.655 --> 00:08:37.855
Their difference becomes our local residual.

08-14
00:08:38.035 --> 00:08:43.966
Write the product-rule steps in B one, rather than jumping straight to
the final equation.

09-01
00:08:46.496 --> 00:08:51.637
Before applying that result to measurements, ask how the measurements
were collected.

09-02
00:08:51.817 --> 00:08:54.791
Temperature, pressure, or both may change.

09-03
00:08:54.971 --> 00:09:00.987
Along a general path, the weighted changes in log gamma include two
additional terms.

09-04
00:09:01.167 --> 00:09:11.002
The temperature term is minus the molar excess enthalpy divided by R
times temperature squared, multiplied by the temperature change.

09-05
00:09:11.182 --> 00:09:18.307
The pressure term is molar excess volume divided by R T, multiplied by
the pressure change.

09-06
00:09:18.487 --> 00:09:22.604
When both temperature and pressure are fixed, both terms vanish.

09-07
00:09:22.784 --> 00:09:29.846
For isothermal vapor-liquid equilibrium, pressure usually varies
across the composition range.

09-08
00:09:30.026 --> 00:09:34.975
Lab zero three neglects the excess-volume pressure contribution.

09-09
00:09:35.155 --> 00:09:41.208
That is an assumption to state, not an exact consequence of holding
temperature fixed.

09-10
00:09:41.388 --> 00:09:44.439
For isobaric data, temperature varies.

09-11
00:09:44.619 --> 00:09:49.099
The excess-enthalpy correction is then needed for the local test.

09-12
00:09:49.279 --> 00:09:57.812
Real data without independent excess-enthalpy information cannot
receive this lab's local consistency verdict.

09-13
00:09:57.992 --> 00:10:04.858
A synthetic generator can supply its own correction, but that is not
independent calorimetry.

10-01
00:10:07.388 --> 00:10:11.078
The methods in this table examine different evidence.

10-02
00:10:11.258 --> 00:10:17.061
The isothermal area method tests an integral over the full composition
range.

10-03
00:10:17.241 --> 00:10:22.766
It needs suitable endpoint information and the stated pressure
approximation.

10-04
00:10:22.946 --> 00:10:27.512
Positive and negative departures can cancel in that integral.

10-05
00:10:27.692 --> 00:10:33.068
The local differential method examines the slope relationship across
composition.

10-06
00:10:33.248 --> 00:10:42.498
It can reveal a localized departure, but differentiation also makes it
sensitive to point spacing, rounding, and noise.

10-07
00:10:42.678 --> 00:10:45.473
Its path corrections must be appropriate.

10-08
00:10:45.653 --> 00:10:51.968
Herington uses an empirical area allowance for an isobaric boiling-
temperature range.

10-09
00:10:52.148 --> 00:10:55.326
It does not provide measured excess enthalpy.

10-10
00:10:55.506 --> 00:11:03.805
A Van Ness-type test instead examines how well a selected model
reproduces pressure and vapor composition.

10-11
00:11:03.985 --> 00:11:08.444
That result depends on the chosen model and the property assumptions.

10-12
00:11:08.624 --> 00:11:10.821
Use precise status words.

10-13
00:11:11.001 --> 00:11:15.481
Not applicable means the method does not match the situation.

10-14
00:11:15.661 --> 00:11:19.352
Not assessed can mean required information is missing.

10-15
00:11:19.532 --> 00:11:23.372
Not run means the calculation has not been performed.

10-16
00:11:23.552 --> 00:11:30.144
Outside this criterion means a calculated diagnostic exceeds its
stated limit.

10-17
00:11:30.324 --> 00:11:35.294
In B three, identify missing information before interpreting a number.

11-01
00:11:37.824 --> 00:11:41.707
Here is the local calculation we will actually use.

11-02
00:11:41.887 --> 00:11:48.714
First, calculate g tilde at every composition using the weighted log
activity coefficients.

11-03
00:11:48.894 --> 00:11:52.028
Second, estimate its slope from neighboring points.

11-04
00:11:52.208 --> 00:11:57.691
Third, subtract L, the center-point log gamma one minus log gamma two.

11-05
00:11:57.871 --> 00:12:05.463
That difference is the residual r, under fixed conditions or the
stated isothermal approximation.

11-06
00:12:05.643 --> 00:12:08.054
The residual is dimensionless.

11-07
00:12:08.234 --> 00:12:17.002
A positive value means the estimated slope exceeds the center log
ratio; a negative value means it is smaller.

11-08
00:12:17.182 --> 00:12:21.470
Its sign alone does not identify a physical cause.

11-09
00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:31.463
Lab zero three summarizes the absolute residuals over interior points
by taking their mean and multiplying by one hundred.

11-10
00:12:31.643 --> 00:12:35.291
The teaching threshold for that scaled score is five.

11-11
00:12:35.471 --> 00:12:40.228
It is not a probability or a percentage of incorrect measurements.

11-12
00:12:40.408 --> 00:12:44.334
One residual is also not the full-data average.

11-13
00:12:44.514 --> 00:12:53.324
Our lab uses three-point finite differences, rather than the NIST or
Kojima point test based on a Padé representation.

11-14
00:12:53.504 --> 00:12:54.656
Pause now.

11-15
00:12:54.836 --> 00:13:00.575
Complete B one to B three on worksheet page one using Lab guide page
one.

11-16
00:13:00.755 --> 00:13:09.454
Allow about fifteen minutes to finish the derivation, path table, and
missing-information explanation before continuing.

12-01
00:13:11.984 --> 00:13:16.144
We can now connect the local slope relationship to an area.

12-02
00:13:16.324 --> 00:13:24.217
Under the chosen pure-liquid standard states, the molar excess Gibbs
energy becomes zero at each pure endpoint.

12-03
00:13:24.397 --> 00:13:30.754
At one endpoint only component two remains; at the other only
component one remains.

12-04
00:13:30.934 --> 00:13:40.961
Integrating L from zero to one is the same as integrating the
derivative of g tilde, provided the slope relationship applies.

12-05
00:13:41.141 --> 00:13:46.880
The integral is therefore the endpoint difference in g tilde: zero
minus zero.

12-06
00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.385
That gives a zero signed area.

12-07
00:13:49.565 --> 00:13:57.552
This is a necessary global check under fixed temperature and pressure,
or under the stated isothermal approximation.

12-08
00:13:57.732 --> 00:14:01.274
It is not sufficient to certify every measurement.

12-09
00:14:01.454 --> 00:14:09.710
Positive and negative contributions can balance even when the
composition-dependent behavior contains a problem.

12-10
00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:15.116
For the exercise, the synthetic endpoint limits are supplied
analytically.

12-11
00:14:15.296 --> 00:14:21.014
In a real experiment, interior points alone would not establish the
missing limits.

13-01
00:14:23.544 --> 00:14:26.680
Complete C one to C three on page two.

13-02
00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:32.236
The table supplies five compositions, with equal spacing of one
quarter.

13-03
00:14:32.416 --> 00:14:39.231
For each adjacent pair, average the two L values and multiply by the
composition interval.

13-04
00:14:39.411 --> 00:14:44.467
Keep the sign of each trapezoid, including the intervals below zero.

13-05
00:14:44.647 --> 00:14:48.636
Write down all four contributions before adding them.

13-06
00:14:48.816 --> 00:14:54.299
Next, add zero point two to every L value and repeat the calculation.

13-07
00:14:54.479 --> 00:15:00.239
You are changing the entire curve vertically, not changing the
composition spacing.

13-08
00:15:00.419 --> 00:15:05.304
Finally, explain why a zero total does not certify the measurements.

13-09
00:15:05.484 --> 00:15:10.754
Refer both to cancellation and to the information required at the
endpoints.

13-10
00:15:10.934 --> 00:15:14.219
Here, the synthetic endpoint limits were supplied.

13-11
00:15:14.399 --> 00:15:18.836
Pause now and allow about eight minutes for C one to C three.

13-12
00:15:19.016 --> 00:15:25.416
Calculate both cases and write your explanation before advancing to
the answer slide.

14-01
00:15:27.946 --> 00:15:40.853
The four baseline trapezoids are zero point three seven five, zero
point one two five, minus zero point one two five, and minus zero
point three seven five.

14-02
00:15:41.033 --> 00:15:44.028
They cancel, so the signed sum is zero.

14-03
00:15:44.208 --> 00:15:51.355
After raising every L value by zero point two, each interval gains
zero point zero five.

14-04
00:15:51.535 --> 00:15:54.970
The new contributions sum to zero point two.

14-05
00:15:55.150 --> 00:16:03.128
This also follows from adding a constant height of zero point two
across a total composition width of one.

14-06
00:16:03.308 --> 00:16:08.791
Our worksheet calls the signed integral S; Lab zero three labels it I.

14-07
00:16:08.971 --> 00:16:13.408
The lab also reports a normalized area imbalance, D.

14-08
00:16:13.588 --> 00:16:24.315
It takes the absolute difference between the positive area and the
magnitude of the negative area, divides by their sum, and multiplies
by one hundred.

14-09
00:16:24.495 --> 00:16:29.210
The negative-area magnitude is entered as a positive quantity.

14-10
00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:34.168
The isothermal teaching criterion is D below five percent.

14-11
00:16:34.348 --> 00:16:38.615
If both areas vanish, that normalization is undefined.

15-01
00:16:41.145 --> 00:16:44.366
Follow C four and Lab guide page one.

15-02
00:16:44.546 --> 00:16:49.261
We will calculate the local residual from the energy values first.

15-03
00:16:49.441 --> 00:16:53.516
These are synthetic values at fixed temperature and pressure.

15-04
00:16:53.696 --> 00:17:04.838
At x equal to zero point four, multiply zero point four by zero point
seven two, then add zero point six times zero point three two.

15-05
00:17:05.018 --> 00:17:07.556
The result is zero point four eight.

15-06
00:17:07.736 --> 00:17:15.523
At the middle composition, both logarithms are zero point five, so
their weighted sum is zero point five.

15-07
00:17:15.703 --> 00:17:21.911
At x equal to zero point six, the weighted sum returns to zero point
four eight.

15-08
00:17:22.091 --> 00:17:26.976
Estimate the slope at the center using the two neighboring energy
values.

15-09
00:17:27.156 --> 00:17:30.932
Their difference is zero, so the centered slope is zero.

15-10
00:17:31.112 --> 00:17:38.020
The center log ratio is also zero, because the two log activity
coefficients there are equal.

15-11
00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:40.888
Subtracting gives a zero residual.

15-12
00:17:41.068 --> 00:17:49.289
The middle energy is still calculated and recorded, although this
equally spaced central slope uses the two neighbors.

15-13
00:17:49.469 --> 00:17:58.749
A zero result at this one center is evidence about this calculation,
not a certificate for an entire experimental data set.

16-01
00:18:01.279 --> 00:18:07.679
Now change only the final log gamma one value, at x equal to zero
point six.

16-02
00:18:07.859 --> 00:18:12.574
Increase it from zero point three two to zero point three six.

16-03
00:18:12.754 --> 00:18:15.868
Leave the other five logarithms unchanged.

16-04
00:18:16.048 --> 00:18:18.630
Recalculate the affected energy.

16-05
00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:29.178
Zero point six times zero point three six, plus zero point four times
zero point seven two, gives zero point five zero four.

16-06
00:18:29.358 --> 00:18:32.963
The left-hand energy remains zero point four eight.

16-07
00:18:33.143 --> 00:18:36.279
Their difference is zero point zero two four.

16-08
00:18:36.459 --> 00:18:43.115
Divide by the composition span of zero point two, giving a slope of
zero point one two.

16-09
00:18:43.295 --> 00:18:49.695
The center log ratio is still zero, so the residual is positive zero
point one two.

16-10
00:18:49.875 --> 00:18:57.854
A neighboring measurement has changed the estimated center slope, even
though the center logarithms have not changed.

16-11
00:18:58.034 --> 00:18:59.954
Record this in C four.

16-12
00:19:00.134 --> 00:19:09.712
With real measurements, a nonzero residual could reflect measurement
error, numerical sensitivity, or an omitted path correction.

16-13
00:19:09.892 --> 00:19:17.423
The residual identifies a departure to investigate; it does not
identify its cause by itself.

17-01
00:19:19.953 --> 00:19:25.350
Now work independently on C five and C six, still on worksheet page
two.

17-02
00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:27.813
Use the new three-row table.

17-03
00:19:27.993 --> 00:19:32.260
First calculate g tilde at each of the three compositions.

17-04
00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:40.333
Then estimate the centered slope at x equal to zero point four using
the left and right energy values.

17-05
00:19:40.513 --> 00:19:43.350
Subtract the log ratio at the center.

17-06
00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:53.600
For the second calculation, change only the final log gamma two value
from zero point three seven five to zero point four two five.

17-07
00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:58.878
Recalculate the affected energy, the center slope, and the residual.

17-08
00:19:59.058 --> 00:20:03.432
Do not change the center log ratio or any other entry.

17-09
00:20:03.612 --> 00:20:15.138
For C six, state why a measured nonzero residual requires further
investigation, and why one center-point result cannot stand for the
full data set.

17-10
00:20:15.318 --> 00:20:20.182
Pause the video now and allow about nine minutes for these two
questions.

17-11
00:20:20.362 --> 00:20:23.988
Finish both calculations before opening the check.

18-01
00:20:26.518 --> 00:20:28.780
Check the energy values first.

18-02
00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:37.642
The clean table gives zero point three one five, zero point three six
zero, and zero point three seven five.

18-03
00:20:37.822 --> 00:20:44.579
The centered slope is the right energy minus the left energy, divided
by zero point two.

18-04
00:20:44.759 --> 00:20:46.914
That gives zero point three.

18-05
00:20:47.094 --> 00:20:54.603
The center log ratio is zero point five four minus zero point two
four, also zero point three.

18-06
00:20:54.783 --> 00:20:57.173
The clean residual is zero.

18-07
00:20:57.353 --> 00:21:03.838
After changing the last log gamma two, the right-hand energy becomes
zero point four.

18-08
00:21:04.018 --> 00:21:07.986
The centered slope is then zero point four two five.

18-09
00:21:08.166 --> 00:21:17.830
Subtract the unchanged center log ratio of zero point three, leaving a
residual of positive zero point one two five.

18-10
00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:22.277
Finite differences depend on spacing, rounding, and noise.

18-11
00:21:22.457 --> 00:21:30.606
Our hand calculation assumes equal spacing; the lab's three-point
formula also handles unequal spacing.

18-12
00:21:30.786 --> 00:21:38.210
Neither a single residual nor a small averaged score establishes a
universal certificate.

18-13
00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:44.022
Keep your corrections, then open Lab guide pages two and three for the
demonstration.

19-01
00:21:46.552 --> 00:21:50.392
We are ready to connect the hand calculations to Lab Three.

19-02
00:21:50.572 --> 00:21:56.076
Keep worksheet page three and Lab guide pages two and three beside the
browser.

19-03
00:21:56.256 --> 00:21:58.837
First we establish a clean reference.

19-04
00:21:59.017 --> 00:22:03.924
Then we introduce two controlled errors, changing one setting at a
time.

19-05
00:22:04.104 --> 00:22:10.525
We compare forty one and one hundred sixty one observations for the
localized error.

19-06
00:22:10.705 --> 00:22:17.383
