27-01
00:34:18.026 --> 00:34:22.293
Compare your completed investigation with these conclusions.

27-02
00:34:22.473 --> 00:34:26.526
The consistent reference has small numerical residuals.

27-03
00:34:26.706 --> 00:34:37.351
The canceling-error case satisfies the area criterion while showing a
much larger local residual and an outside-criterion differential
score.

27-04
00:34:37.531 --> 00:34:48.454
The localized defect is a different lesson: both aggregate scores meet
their teaching thresholds, while the local graph still reveals a
narrow imposed error.

27-05
00:34:48.634 --> 00:35:00.495
Increasing the resolution changes the numerical scores and resolves
the trough more densely; it does not erase the defect or guarantee a
different threshold decision.

27-06
00:35:00.675 --> 00:35:09.849
Finally, the clean-reference NRTL fit reproduces pressure and vapor
composition very closely under its assumptions.

27-07
00:35:10.029 --> 00:35:13.570
That result answers a model-reproduction question.

27-08
00:35:13.750 --> 00:35:21.259
It does not override the need to examine the data path, local
evidence, or missing correction properties.

27-09
00:35:21.439 --> 00:35:37.247
A useful E two conclusion might state that an area result alone cannot
rule out canceling errors, cite your area and local scores, and
identify the synthetic setting and finite-difference approximation as
limitations.

27-10
00:35:37.427 --> 00:35:40.819
Keep any correction beside the original attempt.

27-11
00:35:40.999 --> 00:35:48.679
We now move from checking property relationships to deciding which
phase arrangement minimizes Gibbs energy.

28-01
00:35:51.209 --> 00:35:53.300
Take a ten-minute break now.

28-02
00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:57.960
Save your worksheet and Lab Three evidence before leaving the screen.

28-03
00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:03.900
This break is part of the approximately three-hour lesson, not an
additional assignment.

28-04
00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:07.344
When you return, have worksheet page four ready.

28-05
00:36:07.524 --> 00:36:15.545
We will begin with the distinction between a locally stable
homogeneous liquid and the globally stable phase arrangement.

28-06
00:36:15.725 --> 00:36:24.344
You will calculate phase amounts by hand, then use Lab Four to compare
three overall compositions at the same model setting.

28-07
00:36:24.524 --> 00:36:28.471
The player will remain paused until you choose to continue.

29-01
00:36:31.001 --> 00:36:33.006
We now change the question.

29-02
00:36:33.186 --> 00:36:40.887
Consistency asks whether the composition dependence of the properties
obeys a thermodynamic relationship.

29-03
00:36:41.067 --> 00:36:47.425
Stability asks which admissible arrangement of phases has the lowest
total Gibbs energy.

29-04
00:36:47.605 --> 00:36:57.013
At fixed temperature and pressure, compare one homogeneous liquid with
possible splits that preserve the total amount of each component.

29-05
00:36:57.193 --> 00:37:08.435
The mixing Gibbs energy contains the ideal mixing terms, x log x and
one minus x times log one minus x, plus the excess Gibbs energy.

29-06
00:37:08.615 --> 00:37:15.165
A sufficiently unfavorable excess contribution can make the total
curve nonconvex.

29-07
00:37:15.345 --> 00:37:20.721
The homogeneous liquid can then sit above a lower-energy two-liquid
mixture.

29-08
00:37:20.901 --> 00:37:26.554
Nothing in that statement requires the activity model to violate
Gibbs-Duhem.

29-09
00:37:26.734 --> 00:37:35.331
Indeed, a model derived consistently from one excess Gibbs-energy
function can predict liquid-liquid separation.

29-10
00:37:35.511 --> 00:37:44.130
For F one, explain why checking a property identity does not tell us
whether one liquid is the stable phase arrangement.

30-01
00:37:46.660 --> 00:37:53.849
Local curvature describes how the homogeneous liquid responds to a
small composition fluctuation.

30-02
00:37:54.029 --> 00:38:01.176
Positive curvature means that sufficiently small fluctuations increase
its bulk Gibbs energy.

30-03
00:38:01.356 --> 00:38:06.519
Negative curvature means that small fluctuations can lower the energy.

30-04
00:38:06.699 --> 00:38:13.043
However, a small-fluctuation test does not compare every possible
finite separation.

30-05
00:38:13.223 --> 00:38:18.301
That distinction separates stable and metastable homogeneous liquids.

30-06
00:38:18.481 --> 00:38:22.491
A stable liquid lies on the lower convex envelope.

30-07
00:38:22.671 --> 00:38:30.245
A metastable liquid has positive curvature but lies above a lower-
energy combination of two liquids.

30-08
00:38:30.425 --> 00:38:37.123
An unstable liquid has negative curvature and also fails the global
minimum requirement.

30-09
00:38:37.303 --> 00:38:45.922
For the one-parameter Margules model, the dimensionless excess Gibbs
energy is A times x times one minus x.

30-10
00:38:46.102 --> 00:38:55.275
The curvature of the total mixing function is one divided by the
product of x and one minus x, then subtract two times A.

30-11
00:38:55.455 --> 00:38:57.845
We will use A equal to three.

30-12
00:38:58.025 --> 00:39:07.838
This bulk equilibrium model does not calculate nucleation barriers,
interfaces, or how long a metastable liquid will persist.

31-01
00:39:10.368 --> 00:39:15.445
Imagine joining two points on the Gibbs-energy curve with a straight
line.

31-02
00:39:15.625 --> 00:39:26.527
The energy of a mixture of those two liquid compositions lies on that
line, because the total molar energy is the phase-fraction-weighted
average.

31-03
00:39:26.707 --> 00:39:32.979
At a candidate equilibrium pair, the line has the same slope as the
curve at both ends.

31-04
00:39:33.159 --> 00:39:39.559
That common-tangent condition corresponds to equality of the component
chemical potentials.

31-05
00:39:39.739 --> 00:39:44.389
But finding a stationary pair is only part of the calculation.

31-06
00:39:44.569 --> 00:39:50.884
The tangent must support the whole admitted curve: nowhere may another
composition lie below it.

31-07
00:39:51.064 --> 00:40:00.579
If the candidate line crosses above the curve, another admissible
state can lower the energy and the candidate is not globally stable.

31-08
00:40:00.759 --> 00:40:04.215
Also check that the two compositions are distinct.

31-09
00:40:04.395 --> 00:40:10.112
A solver returning the same composition twice has not established two
liquids.

31-10
00:40:10.292 --> 00:40:20.057
Complete F three by separating these checks: distinct phases,
chemical-potential equality, global support, and compatible amounts.

31-11
00:40:20.237 --> 00:40:25.250
Their material balance cannot substitute for the global energy
comparison.

32-01
00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:30.660
This is the hand-calculation work stop.

32-02
00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:35.918
Complete F two on worksheet page four before seeing the next slide.

32-03
00:40:36.098 --> 00:40:41.666
The supplied endpoints are zero point two zero and zero point eight
zero.

32-04
00:40:41.846 --> 00:40:46.454
Treat their common tangent as globally supported for this exercise.

32-05
00:40:46.634 --> 00:40:51.498
First use an overall composition of zero point five zero.

32-06
00:40:51.678 --> 00:40:54.579
Then repeat for zero point three five.

32-07
00:40:54.759 --> 00:41:06.434
The fraction of the component-one-rich liquid, beta, equals the
overall composition minus the alpha endpoint, divided by the distance
between the two endpoints.

32-08
00:41:06.614 --> 00:41:10.604
Here beta is a liquid label, not a vapor fraction.

32-09
00:41:10.784 --> 00:41:15.456
These illustrative endpoints belong only to the hand exercise.

32-10
00:41:15.636 --> 00:41:20.756
They are not the endpoints that Lab Four must produce for A equal to
three.

32-11
00:41:20.936 --> 00:41:26.632
Pause for about eight minutes, complete both balances, and then
continue to the check.

33-01
00:41:29.162 --> 00:41:36.031
For the feed at zero point five zero, the overall composition is
halfway between the two endpoints.

33-02
00:41:36.211 --> 00:41:40.307
The two liquid fractions are therefore equal: one half each.

33-03
00:41:40.487 --> 00:41:50.706
For the feed at zero point three five, the beta fraction is zero point
one five divided by zero point six zero, or one quarter.

33-04
00:41:50.886 --> 00:41:53.446
The alpha fraction is three quarters.

33-05
00:41:53.626 --> 00:42:05.146
Substituting these amounts gives three quarters times zero point two
zero plus one quarter times zero point eight zero, which reconstructs
zero point three five.

33-06
00:42:05.326 --> 00:42:07.480
The endpoints have not changed.

33-07
00:42:07.660 --> 00:42:19.479
Moving the overall composition inside the same tie line changes how
much of each liquid is present, rather than changing the compositions
of the coexisting liquids.

33-08
00:42:19.659 --> 00:42:28.256
If you insert a feed outside the endpoint interval, the expression
produces a negative fraction or a fraction above one.

33-09
00:42:28.436 --> 00:42:33.514
That is a warning that this two-phase construction does not represent
the feed.

34-01
00:42:36.044 --> 00:42:42.657
Before opening a named Lab Four preset, write your G one predictions
on worksheet page four.

34-02
00:42:42.837 --> 00:42:47.274
The model is one-parameter Margules, with A fixed at three.

34-03
00:42:47.454 --> 00:42:55.433
Compare overall compositions zero point zero two, zero point one two,
and zero point five zero.

34-04
00:42:55.613 --> 00:43:02.141
For each, predict the homogeneous-feed classification and the number
of liquids at equilibrium.

34-05
00:43:02.321 --> 00:43:04.497
These are two separate columns.

34-06
00:43:04.677 --> 00:43:12.442
You can calculate the curvature as one divided by the product of z and
one minus z, then subtract six.

34-07
00:43:12.622 --> 00:43:16.761
A negative value gives a useful local diagnosis.

34-08
00:43:16.941 --> 00:43:21.229
A positive value leaves a global question unresolved.

34-09
00:43:21.409 --> 00:43:26.895
It does not, by itself, distinguish a stable feed from a metastable
one.

34-10
00:43:27.075 --> 00:43:30.958
Write your reasoning before the interface supplies the labels.

34-11
00:43:31.138 --> 00:43:40.016
The presets are named Stable, Metastable, and Unstable, so opening
them before predicting would remove the purpose of this step.

34-12
00:43:40.196 --> 00:43:42.542
Pause now for about three minutes.

34-13
00:43:42.722 --> 00:43:46.776
Complete G one before continuing to the demonstration.

34-14
00:43:46.956 --> 00:43:52.822
This prediction time is part of your Lab Four activity, not an
additional assignment.

35-01
00:43:55.352 --> 00:44:02.051
Now watch the complete Lab Four workflow, from the first input to
reopening saved results.

35-02
00:44:02.231 --> 00:44:07.010
Keep Lab guide page four beside worksheet pages four and five.

35-03
00:44:07.190 --> 00:44:10.560
Your G one predictions should already be written.

35-04
00:44:10.740 --> 00:44:13.599
These are actual views of the working lab.

35-05
00:44:13.779 --> 00:44:18.131
You will repeat the calculations yourself at the next work stop.

35-06
00:44:18.311 --> 00:44:21.340
Open Lab Four and scroll to the workbench.

35-07
00:44:21.520 --> 00:44:24.336
Select Margules, one parameter.

35-08
00:44:24.516 --> 00:44:29.295
Set A to three and the feed mole fraction to zero point zero two.

35-09
00:44:29.475 --> 00:44:32.227
Click Find equilibrium after typing.

35-10
00:44:32.407 --> 00:44:36.354
Keep the Gibbs energy and common tangent view selected.

35-11
00:44:36.534 --> 00:44:38.539
The badge says one liquid.

35-12
00:44:38.719 --> 00:44:45.755
The homogeneous feed is stable, its curvature is positive, and the
Gibbs-energy decrease is zero.

35-13
00:44:45.935 --> 00:44:49.114
The beta fraction is marked not applicable.

35-14
00:44:49.294 --> 00:44:57.742
This is a hypothetical model at fixed temperature and pressure; A is
dimensionless and no physical temperature is assigned.

35-15
00:44:57.922 --> 00:45:01.100
Scroll below the plot to read the boundary note.

35-16
00:45:01.280 --> 00:45:11.324
The interface still lists the binodal compositions, approximately zero
point zero seven zero seven and zero point nine two nine three.

35-17
00:45:11.504 --> 00:45:15.835
Those are boundaries of the miscibility gap for these parameters.

35-18
00:45:16.015 --> 00:45:19.002
They are not two liquids present in this feed.

35-19
00:45:19.182 --> 00:45:25.364
The explicit result is one liquid at x equal to z, or zero point zero
two.

35-20
00:45:25.544 --> 00:45:32.051
The common-tangent segment is a feasible two-liquid mixture only
between its contact points.

35-21
00:45:32.231 --> 00:45:34.578
Open Verify the equilibrium.

35-22
00:45:34.758 --> 00:45:44.571
Here the homogeneous and equilibrium Gibbs energies coincide, at
approximately negative zero point zero three nine two three nine.

35-23
00:45:44.751 --> 00:45:47.183
The balance residual is zero.

35-24
00:45:47.363 --> 00:45:55.747
A chemical-potential equality between two present phases is not
applicable because only one liquid is present.

35-25
00:45:55.927 --> 00:46:04.482
Record the classification and phase count separately, then click
Export study JSON to save this first case.

35-26
00:46:04.662 --> 00:46:08.736
Return to the feed field and enter zero point one two.

35-27
00:46:08.916 --> 00:46:12.180
Leave A at three and click Find equilibrium.

35-28
00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:17.779
On the plot, the homogeneous feed lies above the equilibrium envelope.

35-29
00:46:17.959 --> 00:46:25.148
A finite split can lower the bulk Gibbs energy even though a small
composition fluctuation cannot.

35-30
00:46:25.328 --> 00:46:27.632
Read the updated scorecards.

35-31
00:46:27.812 --> 00:46:33.466
The homogeneous feed is metastable and the equilibrium badge says two
liquids.

35-32
00:46:33.646 --> 00:46:37.720
The curvature is positive, about three point four seven.

35-33
00:46:37.900 --> 00:46:42.530
The beta fraction is about zero point zero five seven four.

35-34
00:46:42.710 --> 00:46:51.142
The energy decrease, divided by R T, is about zero point zero zero
eight two one six per mole of feed.

35-35
00:46:51.322 --> 00:46:57.424
This case shows why positive curvature alone cannot establish global
stability.

35-36
00:46:57.604 --> 00:47:03.833
Below the plot, read the two actual coexistence compositions and the
phase-fraction bar.

35-37
00:47:04.013 --> 00:47:09.773
Alpha is the component-one-poor liquid; beta is the component-one-rich
liquid.

35-38
00:47:09.953 --> 00:47:16.566
The fractions are about zero point nine four two six and zero point
zero five seven four.

35-39
00:47:16.746 --> 00:47:20.672
These are amounts of phases, not their compositions.

35-40
00:47:20.852 --> 00:47:29.300
In your independent work, use the lever rule with the actual
endpoints, then reconstruct the feed from the weighted balance.

35-41
00:47:29.480 --> 00:47:34.621
Keep more digits from the exported results when checking numerical
agreement.

35-42
00:47:34.801 --> 00:47:39.409
Under Verify the equilibrium, inspect the supporting evidence.

35-43
00:47:39.589 --> 00:47:54.591
The homogeneous energy is approximately negative zero point zero five
zero one two five; the equilibrium energy is lower, approximately
negative zero point zero five eight three four one.

35-44
00:47:54.771 --> 00:47:58.099
Their difference matches the energy-decrease card.

35-45
00:47:58.279 --> 00:48:06.023
The balance is zero and the chemical-potential and common-tangent
residuals are near numerical precision.

35-46
00:48:06.203 --> 00:48:13.008
Inspect the whole curve as well: a small residual alone is not proof
of global stability.

35-47
00:48:13.188 --> 00:48:20.186
The minimum tangent-plane distance shown here is a separate
diagnostic, not the energy decrease.

35-48
00:48:20.366 --> 00:48:24.078
Export this second study before changing the feed.

35-49
00:48:24.258 --> 00:48:29.079
For the third case, choose the unstable example under Explore a state.

35-50
00:48:29.259 --> 00:48:35.638
This preset sets A to three and z to zero point five zero, and
calculates immediately.

35-51
00:48:35.818 --> 00:48:39.956
The feed has negative curvature, equal to negative two.

35-52
00:48:40.136 --> 00:48:47.923
The equilibrium is two liquids, and the Gibbs-energy decrease is about
zero point one one five one nine four.

35-53
00:48:48.103 --> 00:48:51.602
Read the phase-fraction bar for this central feed.

35-54
00:48:51.782 --> 00:48:59.398
Both fractions are one half, while the coexistence compositions remain
the same as in the previous two-liquid case.

35-55
00:48:59.578 --> 00:49:05.935
Changing the feed inside this fixed miscibility gap changes the
amounts of the liquids.

35-56
00:49:06.115 --> 00:49:09.720
It does not change their equilibrium compositions.

35-57
00:49:09.900 --> 00:49:11.884
Export this third study.

35-58
00:49:12.064 --> 00:49:15.819
Open Study workspace, reopen and compare cases.

35-59
00:49:15.999 --> 00:49:21.375
Each calculator export has retained a case in this in-memory table.

35-60
00:49:21.555 --> 00:49:30.808
Case one has feed zero point zero two, case two has zero point one
two, and case three has zero point five zero.

35-61
00:49:30.988 --> 00:49:33.335
Compare the state and phase rows.

35-62
00:49:33.515 --> 00:49:42.923
The binodal boundary fields appear even for the one-liquid case, so
use the actual phase result when reporting which liquids are present.

35-63
00:49:43.103 --> 00:49:47.540
An absent phase fraction is not a zero-valued measurement.

35-64
00:49:47.720 --> 00:49:54.014
Click Download comparison to save all three cases in one JSON file.

35-65
00:49:54.194 --> 00:50:01.596
This downloaded file is the durable copy; the in-memory table alone is
not your saved submission.

35-66
00:50:01.776 --> 00:50:08.667
For this demonstration, I have downloaded the file and then cleared
only the in-memory comparison.

35-67
00:50:08.847 --> 00:50:11.684
The downloaded file remains unchanged.

35-68
00:50:11.864 --> 00:50:17.987
Use Load studies or a comparison JSON and choose that downloaded file.

35-69
00:50:18.167 --> 00:50:20.706
All three recorded cases return.

35-70
00:50:20.886 --> 00:50:24.939
Select Restore Case two to load its input controls.

35-71
00:50:25.119 --> 00:50:33.183
